The '''Ministry of Finance''' ({{lang-ja|財務省}}, {{Nihongo|Zaimu-shō|ざいむしょう}}) is a [[Cabinet of Okaiken|cabinet-level ministry]] of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for the formulation of [[fiscal policy]], management of the [[national budget]], oversight of the [[tax system|taxation system]], regulation of [[financial markets]], and administration of [[government debt]]. It is one of the most powerful and influential ministries in the Okaiken government, often described as the "nerve centre" of the state's economic apparatus.

The ministry oversees a national budget of {{currency|1.6 trillion|SEN}} in expenditure against {{currency|1.3 trillion|SEN}} in revenue (FY 2025), and manages a public debt burden equivalent to 236% of Okaiken's [[Gross domestic product|GDP]] — the highest ratio among major developed economies. Its portfolio includes the [[Bank of Okaiken]] (the central bank), the [[Okami Stock Exchange]], the [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]], the [[National Tax Agency (Okaiken)|National Tax Agency]], and the [[Customs and Tariff Bureau (Okaiken)|Customs and Tariff Bureau]].

The current Minister of Finance is [[Kazuya Fujita]] of the [[Social Civic Party (Okaiken)|Social Civic Party]] (SCP), who assumed office on 20 May 2026 as part of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. His predecessor was [[Satsuki Katayama]] of the [[People's Party of Okaiken|People's Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), who served from August 2021 to May 2026 under the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]].

==History==

===Imperial era (1869–1945)===
The Ministry of Finance traces its origins to the {{Nihongo|Ōkura-shō|大蔵省}}, established in 1869 during the [[Meiji-era reforms (Okaiken)|Meiji-era reforms]] that modernised the Sanese state apparatus. Modeled partly on the British [[HM Treasury]] and partly on the French [[Ministry of Economy and Finance (France)|Ministry of Finance]], the Ōkura-shō was initially responsible for tax collection, currency issuance, and the management of state assets. Its first minister, {{Nihongo|Tadakatsu Inoue|井上忠勝|Inoue Tadakatsu}}, oversaw the introduction of the [[Sanese En|En]] as the national currency in 1871, unifying the patchwork of feudal currencies that had characterised the pre-modern economy.

Throughout the imperial period, the ministry wielded enormous influence over economic policy, often eclipsing the Cabinet in fiscal matters. The {{Nihongo|Budget Bureau|主計局|Shukei-kyoku}} became the de facto arbiter of public spending, with its officials — drawn from the elite ranks of the [[University of Okami|Imperial University of Okami]] — exercising what critics termed "bureaucratic supremacy" ({{Nihongo|官僚支配|kanryō shihai}}). The ministry's role in financing the [[Sanese imperial expansion]] and the subsequent war effort led to significant expansion of its staff and powers during the 1930s and early 1940s.

===Post-war period (1945–2001)===
Following the end of [[World War II]], the ministry was reorganised under the [[Occupation of Okaiken|post-war occupation authorities]], who sought to democratise the bureaucracy while retaining its technical competence. The ministry retained its original name Ōkura-shō until the [[2001 Central Government Reform]], which restructured the bureaucracy and renamed it {{Nihongo|Zaimu-shō|財務省}} to reflect its modernised mandate.

The post-war decades saw the ministry at the centre of Okaiken's remarkable economic growth. During the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]] (1955–1973), the ministry coordinated industrial policy with the [[Ministry of International Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|Ministry of International Trade and Industry]] and the [[Bank of Okaiken]], directing credit to strategic sectors and maintaining a deliberately undervalued En to boost exports. The so-called "iron triangle" of bureaucrats, politicians, and business leaders defined this era.

The collapse of the [[Okami asset bubble]] in 1991 marked a turning point. The ministry was widely criticised for its failure to anticipate the bubble and for its sluggish response to the ensuing "Lost Decade." Fiscal stimulus packages totalling over {{currency|100 trillion|SEN}} were deployed, but their effectiveness was debated, and public debt began its inexorable rise from approximately 60% of GDP in 1990 to over 150% by 2000.

===2001 Central Government Reform===
The [[2001 Central Government Reform]] ({{Nihongo|中央省庁再編|Chūō Shōchō Saihen}}) was the most significant restructuring of the Sanese bureaucracy since the post-war occupation. The Ministry of Finance was renamed from Ōkura-shō to Zaimu-shō, and its financial regulatory functions were partially transferred to the newly created [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]] (FSA), which was established as an external bureau of the ministry. The reform also merged several smaller agencies into the ministry and reorganised its internal bureaus.

Critics argued that the reform did not go far enough, noting that the FSA remained under the ministry's administrative umbrella and that the ministry retained effective control over financial regulation. Supporters countered that the reform improved transparency and accountability while preserving the institutional knowledge and technical expertise that made the ministry effective.

===Modern era (2001–present)===
The 21st century has been defined by the ministry's struggle to manage Okaiken's mounting fiscal challenges. The [[Aoyagi administration (Okaiken)|Aoyagi administration]] (2014–2021) introduced the controversial "Wealth Solidarity Surcharge" ({{Nihongo|富の連帯追加税|Tomi no Rendai Tsuikazei}}), a progressive wealth tax on high-net-worth individuals and large estates, which generated significant revenue but drew fierce opposition from business groups and the [[People's Party of Okaiken|PPO]].

Upon taking office in 2021, [[Honda Shigetsuma|Prime Minister Honda]] reversed the Aoyagi surcharge as part of his administration's pro-business economic agenda, arguing that the tax had driven capital flight and discouraged investment. Finance Minister [[Satsuki Katayama]] oversaw the repeal, which took effect in FY 2022, resulting in an estimated {{currency|45 billion|SEN}} annual revenue loss that was partially offset by spending cuts.

The [[En Crisis (2026)|En Crisis]] of January–February 2026, which saw the En depreciate by approximately 20% against the [[TALOD Reserve Unit|TRU]], and the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026, which caused an estimated {{currency|420 billion|SEN}} in economic damage, have placed the ministry under unprecedented pressure. The [[ACTA]] credit downgrade from AAA to AA in May 2026 — the first in Okaiken's modern history — has intensified debate over the ministry's fiscal strategy and the sustainability of the national debt.

==Organization==

The Ministry of Finance is organised into internal bureaus, affiliated agencies, and special institutions. As of 2026, the ministry employs approximately 48,200 personnel across all its divisions.

===Internal bureaus===

;Minister's Secretariat ({{Nihongo|大臣官房|Daijin Kanbō}})
:The Minister's Secretariat coordinates the ministry's overall operations, manages personnel, handles public relations, and serves as the primary interface between the ministry and the Cabinet. It also oversees the ministry's internal audit functions and information security protocols.

;Budget Bureau ({{Nihongo|主計局|Shukei-kyoku}})
:The Budget Bureau is the most powerful division within the ministry, responsible for drafting the national budget and reviewing all government expenditure requests. Its examiners ({{Nihongo|主計官|shukeikan}}) are renowned for their rigorous scrutiny of spending proposals from other ministries, earning the bureau the nickname "the guardian of the treasury." The bureau oversees the {{currency|1.6 trillion|SEN}} expenditure budget and coordinates the annual budget formulation process, which begins each summer and concludes with Diet approval before the start of the fiscal year on 1 April.

;Tax Bureau ({{Nihongo|主税局|Shuzei-kyoku}})
:The Tax Bureau is responsible for the design and administration of the national tax system, including income tax, corporate tax, consumption tax, and other levies. It proposes tax reforms, drafts tax legislation, and coordinates with the [[National Tax Agency (Okaiken)|National Tax Agency]] on enforcement. The bureau also oversees [[Taxation in Okaiken|Okaiken's tax brackets]] and deduction schedules.

;Financial Bureau ({{Nihongo|理財局|Rizai-kyoku}})
:The Financial Bureau manages the government's cash flow, debt issuance, and fiscal investment and loan programmes. It oversees the issuance of [[Government bonds of Okaiken|government bonds]] ({{Nihongo|国債|kokusai}}), which finance the annual fiscal deficit of approximately {{currency|300 billion|SEN}}. The bureau also manages the [[Fiscal Investment and Loan Programme (Okaiken)|Fiscal Investment and Loan Programme]] (FILP), which channels postal savings and pension reserves into public infrastructure and other investments.

;Customs and Tariff Bureau ({{Nihongo|関税局|Kanzei-kyoku}})
:The Customs and Tariff Bureau administers customs policy, tariff rates, and trade facilitation. It works closely with the [[Okaiken Customs]] service and represents Okaiken in international trade negotiations on tariff matters. The bureau also enforces trade sanctions and controls on strategic goods.

;International Bureau ({{Nihongo|国際局|Kokusai-kyoku}})
:The International Bureau handles international financial affairs, including coordination with the [[International Monetary Fund]], the [[World Bank]], and other multilateral institutions. It manages Okaiken's foreign exchange policy in coordination with the [[Bank of Okaiken]] and participates in [[G7]] and [[G20]] financial forums.

;Economic Research and Statistics Bureau ({{Nihongo|経済調査統計局|Keizai Chōsa Tōkei-kyoku}})
:Established during the 2001 reform, this bureau conducts economic research, publishes financial statistics, and provides analytical support for policy formulation. It produces the annual {{Nihongo|Financial and Economic Review|財政経済年報|Zaisei Keizai Nenpō}} and maintains the ministry's economic forecasting models.

==Key agencies==

===Financial Services Agency===
{{Main|Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)}}
The '''Financial Services Agency''' (FSA; {{Nihongo|金融庁|Kin'yū-chō}}) is an external bureau of the ministry responsible for the supervision and regulation of Okaiken's financial sector, including banks, insurance companies, securities firms, and the [[Okami Stock Exchange]]. Established as part of the 2001 Central Government Reform, the FSA was intended to provide independent financial oversight, though it remains administratively under the ministry's umbrella. The FSA has approximately 1,800 employees and is headed by a Commissioner appointed by the Prime Minister on the recommendation of the Minister of Finance.

===National Tax Agency===
{{Main|National Tax Agency (Okaiken)}}
The '''National Tax Agency''' (NTA; {{Nihongo|国税庁|Kokuzei-chō}}) is responsible for the assessment and collection of national taxes, tax audits, and enforcement of tax law. With approximately 55,000 employees across its headquarters and regional offices, it is the largest agency under the ministry's jurisdiction. The NTA conducts approximately 12,000 corporate tax audits annually and is responsible for investigating major tax evasion cases.

===Okaiken Customs===
{{Main|Okaiken Customs}}
'''Okaiken Customs''' ({{Nihongo|税関|Zeikan}}) operates under the authority of the Customs and Tariff Bureau and is responsible for customs clearance, tariff collection, border control of goods, and enforcement of trade regulations. It maintains customs offices at all major ports and airports, including [[Okami International Port]], [[Sakai Port]], and [[Hakata International Airport]].

===Special institutions===
* '''[[Bank of Okaiken]]''' ({{Nihongo|沖軒銀行|Okaiken Ginkō}}): The central bank, which the ministry oversees in coordination with the Bank's Policy Board. While the Bank of Okaiken Act of 1998 granted the central bank significant operational independence, the ministry retains influence over exchange rate policy and coordinates closely on monetary-fiscal policy alignment.
* '''[[Okami Stock Exchange]]''' ({{Nihongo|岡見証券取引所|Okami Shōken Torihikijo}}): The ministry, through the FSA, exercises regulatory oversight over the Okami Stock Exchange, Okaiken's primary securities exchange.
* '''[[National Institute of Public Finance (Okaiken)|National Institute of Public Finance]]''' ({{Nihongo|財政研究所|Zaisei Kenkyūjo}}): A research institute that conducts studies on fiscal policy, public finance, and tax systems.

==Budget and fiscal policy==

===National budget===
The Ministry of Finance is responsible for the formulation, execution, and oversight of the [[Budget of Okaiken|national budget]]. For [[Fiscal year|FY]] 2025, the budget comprises {{currency|1.6 trillion|SEN}} in expenditure against {{currency|1.3 trillion|SEN}} in revenue, resulting in a primary deficit of approximately {{currency|300 billion|SEN}}. The budget is formulated through an annual process in which the Budget Bureau reviews requests from all government ministries and agencies before the Minister of Finance presents the draft budget to the [[Diet of Okaiken|National Diet]] for deliberation and approval.

The largest expenditure categories include social security (approximately 33% of the budget), public debt servicing (approximately 24%), local government fiscal transfers (approximately 16%), and public works (approximately 11%). Defence spending, education, and economic stimulus measures account for the remainder.

===Debt management===
Okaiken's [[Government debt of Okaiken|gross public debt]] stands at approximately 236% of GDP ({{currency|9.27 trillion|SEN}}), the highest ratio among major developed economies. However, approximately 90% of this debt is held domestically — primarily by the [[Bank of Okaiken]], the [[Okaiken Postal Bank]], domestic insurance companies, and individual investors — which has insulated Okaiken from the sovereign debt crises that have affected other highly indebted nations.

The ministry manages debt issuance through the Financial Bureau, which conducts regular auctions of government bonds with maturities ranging from 2 years to 40 years. The long-term interest rate on 10-year government bonds has remained below 1.5% for most of the past two decades, reflecting strong domestic demand and the Bank of Okaiken's accommodative monetary policy.

The sustainability of Okaiken's debt burden remains a subject of intense debate. The [[ACTA]] credit downgrade from AAA to AA in May 2026, following the [[Satori AI Blackout]], cited "structural fiscal vulnerabilities and the diminishing capacity to absorb fiscal shocks" as key factors. The ministry has maintained that the domestic orientation of debt holdings and the En-denominated nature of the obligations ensure continued debt sustainability.

===Fiscal rules and targets===
The ministry operates under a set of fiscal guidelines established by the [[Fiscal Sustainability Act (Okaiken)|Fiscal Sustainability Act]] of 2017, which set a target of achieving a primary balance surplus by FY 2030. However, this target has been repeatedly deferred, and the current fiscal trajectory suggests it will not be met without significant policy changes. The ministry publishes a semi-annual [[Fiscal Strategy Report]] outlining progress toward fiscal targets and projected debt trajectories.

===Revenue and taxation===
{{Main|Taxation in Okaiken}}
Government revenue of {{currency|1.3 trillion|SEN}} is derived primarily from three sources: income tax (approximately 35% of revenue), consumption tax (approximately 30%), and corporate tax (approximately 18%). The remaining revenue comes from customs duties, stamp taxes, and other miscellaneous levies. The ministry's Tax Bureau is responsible for the design and periodic revision of [[Taxation in Okaiken|tax brackets]], deduction schedules, and special levies.

The [[Aoyagi administration]]'s "Wealth Solidarity Surcharge" (2018–2021) represented a significant departure from traditional revenue policy, imposing a progressive wealth tax on net assets exceeding {{currency|500 million|SEN}} at rates ranging from 0.5% to 3.0%. The surcharge generated approximately {{currency|45 billion|SEN}} annually before its repeal by the Honda administration in 2022.

==Key legislation==

The Ministry of Finance administers and enforces numerous statutes governing fiscal policy, taxation, and financial regulation. Key legislation includes:

* '''[[Budget Law (Okaiken)|Budget Law]]''' ({{Nihongo|予算法|Yosan-hō}}, 1947): Establishes the procedures for budget formulation, Diet deliberation, and execution. Requires the government to submit a balanced budget proposal or provide justification for any deficit.
* '''[[Public Finance Act (Okaiken)|Public Finance Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|財政法|Zaisei-hō}}, 1947): Governs the management of public funds, government accounting, and debt issuance. Establishes the principle that government bonds may only be issued to finance capital expenditure or to cover deficits authorised by the Diet.
* '''[[Financial Instruments and Exchange Act (Okaiken)|Financial Instruments and Exchange Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|金融商品取引法|Kin'yū Shōhin Torihiki-hō}}, 1948, extensively amended 2006): The primary statute governing securities regulation, investor protection, and financial market oversight. Administered jointly with the FSA.
* '''[[Income Tax Act (Okaiken)|Income Tax Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|所得税法|Shotokuzei-hō}}, 1965): Establishes the progressive income tax structure, deduction schedules, and filing requirements for individuals.
* '''[[Corporation Tax Act (Okaiken)|Corporation Tax Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|法人税法|Hōjinzei-hō}}, 1965): Governs the taxation of corporate income, including rates, deductions, and international tax rules.
* '''[[Bank of Okaiken Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|沖軒銀行法|Okaiken Ginkō-hō}}, 1998): Establishes the independence of the central bank while preserving the ministry's role in exchange rate policy and international financial coordination.
* '''[[Fiscal Sustainability Act (Okaiken)|Fiscal Sustainability Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|財政持続可能性法|Zaisei Jizoku Kanōsei-hō}}, 2017): Sets medium-term fiscal targets and requires the government to publish regular fiscal strategy reports.

==Recent events==

===The En Crisis (January–February 2026)===
{{Main|En Crisis (2026)}}
The [[En Crisis (2026)|En Crisis]] of January–February 2026 was a period of severe currency depreciation in which the Sanese En lost approximately 20% of its value against the [[TALOD Reserve Unit|TRU]] and other major currencies. Triggered by a combination of widening fiscal deficits, political uncertainty surrounding the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]], and speculative pressure from international hedge funds, the crisis represented the most significant currency shock in Okaiken since the 1990s.

The Ministry of Finance, in coordination with the [[Bank of Okaiken]], intervened in foreign exchange markets on multiple occasions, spending an estimated {{currency|8.2 billion|SEN}} in reserves to stabilise the currency. Finance Minister [[Satsuki Katayama]] held emergency press conferences and announced a package of fiscal consolidation measures, including a 5% reduction in discretionary spending and the acceleration of previously scheduled consumption tax increases. The crisis abated in late February after the Bank of Okaiken raised its policy rate and the ministry announced a credible medium-term fiscal consolidation plan.

===The Satori AI Blackout (May 2026)===
{{Main|Satori AI Blackout}}
The [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026 was a catastrophic failure of the [[Satori AI]] system — Okaiken's flagship artificial intelligence infrastructure — which caused widespread disruption to financial markets, payment systems, and government services. The economic damage was estimated at {{currency|420 billion|SEN}}, making it the costliest technological disaster in Sanese history.

The Ministry of Finance coordinated the emergency fiscal response, authorising emergency appropriations and working with the FSA to stabilise financial institutions affected by the outage. The Okami Stock Exchange was closed for two trading days — the first unscheduled closure since the 2011 earthquake — and the ministry implemented temporary capital controls to prevent a run on the banking system.

===ACTA credit downgrade (May 2026)===
In the immediate aftermath of the Satori AI Blackout, the [[ACTA]] (Agency for Credit and Trade Assessment) downgraded Okaiken's sovereign credit rating from AAA to AA, the first downgrade in the country's modern history. ACTA cited "the compounding effect of the Blackout on an already fragile fiscal position, the structural deficit, and the elevated debt-to-GDP ratio of 236%" as the primary reasons for the downgrade.

The downgrade sent shockwaves through Sanese financial markets, with government bond yields rising by 30 basis points on the first trading day after the announcement. Finance Minister-designate [[Kazuya Fujita]], who was preparing to assume office as part of the incoming [[Ikazaki Cabinet]], described the downgrade as "a wake-up call that demands urgent and credible fiscal action." The ministry subsequently announced the formation of a '''Special Fiscal Resilience Task Force''' ({{Nihongo|特別財政レジリエンスタスクフォース|Tokubetsu Zaisei Rejiriensu Tasukufōsu}}) to develop a comprehensive response plan.

==Controversies==

===2024 Budget Manipulation Scandal===
In March 2024, the [[Okaiken Shimbun]] newspaper reported that officials in the Budget Bureau had systematically manipulated revenue projections in the FY 2023 and FY 2024 budget proposals to understate the projected deficit, thereby making the fiscal position appear more favourable to the Diet and the public. An internal investigation, ordered by Finance Minister Katayama, confirmed that senior examiners had applied overly optimistic GDP growth assumptions and had deferred the recognition of {{currency|38 billion|SEN}} in contingent liabilities.

The scandal led to the resignation of Budget Bureau Director-General {{Nihongo|Kenji Ōta|太田健司|Ōta Kenji}} and the disciplinary transfer of five senior examiners. The opposition [[Social Civic Party (Okaiken)|SCP]] called for Katayama's resignation, but Prime Minister Honda expressed confidence in her leadership, and she remained in post until the change of government in May 2026.

A subsequent investigation by the [[Board of Audit of Okaiken|Board of Audit]] found that the manipulation had been facilitated by the lack of independent verification of budget assumptions and recommended the establishment of an independent '''Fiscal Forecasting Committee''' ({{Nihongo|財政予測委員会|Zaisei Yosoku Iinkai}}) to review and validate the ministry's economic projections. The Katayama ministry accepted the recommendation in principle, but legislation to establish the committee was not passed before the end of the Honda administration.

===Wealth Solidarity Surcharge debate===
The Aoyagi administration's "Wealth Solidarity Surcharge" (2018–2021) and its subsequent repeal by the Honda administration (2022) remain one of the most politically divisive fiscal issues in Sanese politics. Supporters of the surcharge argue that it was a necessary measure of progressive taxation that addressed growing wealth inequality and generated revenue for social programmes. Critics contend that it drove capital flight, discouraged entrepreneurship, and was administratively burdensome.

Finance Minister Katayama's handling of the repeal drew criticism from both sides: from the left for abandoning a progressive revenue source, and from the right for not replacing the lost revenue with offsetting spending cuts. The {{currency|45 billion|SEN}} annual revenue gap contributed to the widening fiscal deficit during the Honda years.

===Tax evasion enforcement===
The National Tax Agency has faced recurring criticism over its enforcement practices. In 2022, a high-profile investigation revealed that {{currency|12 billion|SEN}} in corporate tax had been evaded through the use of shell companies in offshore jurisdictions by a consortium of Sanese trading firms. The NTA's response was criticised as slow and inadequate, with opposition legislators accusing the agency of being "more aggressive against small business owners than against large corporations with the resources to exploit loopholes."

